# Shell commands — no chaining

This environment uses **Windows PowerShell**. Chained shell syntax often fails or behaves unexpectedly and wastes time.

## Do not use

In the Shell tool `command` string, **never** use:

- `&&` or `||` (bash-style chaining — unreliable or invalid in PowerShell)
- One-liners like `cd path && npm test` or `git add . && git commit`

Prefer **one command per Shell call** instead of chaining with `;` as well.

## Do instead

1. **Separate Shell tool calls** — one command per invocation. Use `working_directory` instead of `cd`.
2. **Parallel calls** when commands are independent (e.g. `git status`, `git diff`, `git log` together).
3. **Sequential calls** when step B depends on step A — run A, read the result, then run B.

## Examples

```text
# Bad
git add src/foo.py && git commit -m "fix"

# Good — two Shell calls; second only after the first succeeds
git add src/foo.py
git commit -m "fix: ..."
```

```text
# Bad
cd tests && pytest test_foo.py

# Good
pytest test_foo.py
# with working_directory set to the tests folder (or repo root as appropriate)
```

If a command fails, stop and fix or report — do not chain “run only if previous succeeded” into one string; rely on separate steps.
